1
00:00:13,841 --> 00:00:19,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I found a passage in the suttas that might be of interest to others.

2
00:00:21,023 --> 00:00:21,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I thought I'd...

3
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:29,765
[SPEAKER_00]: It's similar to the sutta on the dart, called the dart or the sick ward.

4
00:00:31,705 --> 00:00:41,988
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Blessed One said this, and how is one undeveloped in body and undeveloped in mind?

5
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:48,253
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a case where a pleasant feeling arises in an ordinary person.

6
00:00:49,313 --> 00:00:57,879
[SPEAKER_00]: On being touched by the pleasant feeling, he becomes impassioned with pleasure and is reduced to being impassioned with pleasure.

7
00:00:59,299 --> 00:01:01,220
[SPEAKER_00]: His pleasant feeling ceases.

8
00:01:02,301 --> 00:01:07,044
[SPEAKER_00]: With the cessation of that pleasant feeling, there arises a painful feeling.

9
00:01:07,965 --> 00:01:11,607
[SPEAKER_00]: On being touched with a painful feeling, he sorrows, grieves,

10
00:01:12,768 --> 00:01:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: becomes distraught.

11
00:01:14,469 --> 00:01:23,076
[SPEAKER_00]: When that pleasant feeling had arisen in him, it invaded his mind and remained because of his lack of development of the body.

12
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:30,941
[SPEAKER_00]: When that painful feeling had arisen in him, it invaded his mind and remained because of his lack of development of the mind.

13
00:01:31,982 --> 00:01:35,124
[SPEAKER_00]: This is how one is undeveloped in body and undeveloped in mind.

14
00:01:37,257 --> 00:01:45,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there's a pleasant feeling, why is it, when there's a pleasant feeling, it invades his mind, because he's undeveloped, undeveloped in body?

15
00:01:48,604 --> 00:01:55,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in regard to, in regard to the pleasure, you need to be developed in the body, or developed in regard to the body.

16
00:01:56,441 --> 00:02:00,723
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, regarding the pain, you need to be developed in regard to the mind.

17
00:02:00,783 --> 00:02:08,127
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is deemed pleasant, pleasant, that which is which is considered agreeable, is that which is obtainable through the senses.

18
00:02:09,047 --> 00:02:10,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's fundamentally bodily.

19
00:02:11,028 --> 00:02:14,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to the body, and pleasure is experienced through the body.

20
00:02:14,891 --> 00:02:16,932
[SPEAKER_01]: The pleasing sight, sound, smell, taste, touch.

21
00:02:17,432 --> 00:02:25,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, your mind might be delighting in the prospect of it, but even then, you want to be touching these pleasures with the body, so to speak.

22
00:02:25,617 --> 00:02:30,660
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be consuming food, enjoying music, or whatever the pleasure you're seeking.

23
00:02:31,261 --> 00:02:32,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is bodily.

24
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:36,624
[SPEAKER_01]: It lands on the body, like the counterpart of the sense object are the sense organs.

25
00:02:37,636 --> 00:02:41,998
[SPEAKER_01]: They're the recipients of what the objects have to offer.

26
00:02:42,998 --> 00:02:46,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And those objects are agreeable and disagreeable.

27
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:49,301
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the same for putujana and for an arahant.

28
00:02:49,401 --> 00:02:50,302
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no difference there.

29
00:02:51,162 --> 00:02:59,746
[SPEAKER_01]: But the difference is that for a non-arahant, for somebody who is not free from sensuality, the pleasure of the senses

30
00:03:00,839 --> 00:03:09,626
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense organs that enjoy the sense objects, is their pleasure, is the pleasure that they delight in, that they cannot remain indifferent towards.

31
00:03:10,707 --> 00:03:14,410
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means, like, being touched by the pleasure, you can't prevent that.

32
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:17,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a pleasing sight, just there isn't.

33
00:03:17,152 --> 00:03:17,632
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw it.

34
00:03:18,093 --> 00:03:18,893
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't prevent that.

35
00:03:18,933 --> 00:03:20,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the touch of the pleasure is there.

36
00:03:22,737 --> 00:03:31,219
[SPEAKER_01]: But that does not necessarily mean that automatically your mind must be craving it, delighting in it, wanting more of it, seeking it out, acting out of it, and everything else.

37
00:03:32,079 --> 00:03:36,080
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, I mean, we said it in so many talks, because the suttas, there's so many suttas about it.

38
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:45,742
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha would say, like, having seen something, heard something, taste something, he doesn't welcome it, he doesn't delight it, he doesn't act out of it, he doesn't entertain it.

39
00:03:45,842 --> 00:03:50,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like he prevents it from being experienced in the first place.

40
00:03:50,963 --> 00:03:51,763
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not possible.

41
00:03:52,858 --> 00:03:58,202
[SPEAKER_01]: You can restrain yourself and contain how much of these things will come your way, so you're not overwhelmed.

42
00:03:58,963 --> 00:04:05,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're constantly bombarded by pressure of the pleasing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, you won't be able to handle it.

43
00:04:05,368 --> 00:04:09,532
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you need to be restrained, withdrawn, but sooner or later something agreeable will arise.

44
00:04:10,653 --> 00:04:16,498
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem is never in those things that have arisen.

45
00:04:16,638 --> 00:04:20,221
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in you not being able to remain unmoved by it.

46
00:04:21,482 --> 00:04:25,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you develop yourself in regard to your own body.

47
00:04:25,463 --> 00:04:28,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the extent of your body is the extent of your sensuality.

48
00:04:29,525 --> 00:04:36,767
[SPEAKER_01]: All these sense objects in the world, the variety of them, all of that is unified by the pleasure that's experienced only through this body.

49
00:04:37,507 --> 00:04:42,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, all you can experience is your sense organs experiencing sense objects.

50
00:04:43,529 --> 00:04:46,950
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no direct line with the sense objects.

51
00:04:47,810 --> 00:04:53,594
[SPEAKER_01]: You do so only when you have fully assumed the ownership and you stop seeing, basically, the sense organs for what they are.

52
00:04:54,235 --> 00:04:56,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Then all you see is me and things I want.

53
00:04:57,157 --> 00:05:04,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But any wanting, any pursuit, any central engagement is only on the basis of the sense organs providing you with it.

54
00:05:06,453 --> 00:05:11,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being developed in regard to the sense organs, means being developed in regard to your own body.

55
00:05:11,415 --> 00:05:15,417
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is within that framework of your six sense bases.

56
00:05:15,557 --> 00:05:26,283
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's often referred to as bases of fields, the ayatanas, in the suttas, through which the sights manifest.

57
00:05:27,683 --> 00:05:30,325
[SPEAKER_01]: But people are obviously generally not aware

58
00:05:31,973 --> 00:05:35,935
[SPEAKER_01]: There are sense organs in the true nature, so all they see is just sense objects.

59
00:05:36,495 --> 00:05:48,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, whether it's sensuality pleasing me, or aversion displeasing me, and so on, it's all projected out there in the world, like, oh, this is the cause of it, this is what upsets me, this is what pleases me.

60
00:05:49,861 --> 00:05:57,584
[SPEAKER_01]: But fundamentally, it's just your eyes are upset, or displeased, or pleased and delighting in something.

61
00:05:58,614 --> 00:05:59,234
[SPEAKER_01]: That they've seen.

62
00:05:59,614 --> 00:06:11,478
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole simile of the five animals, whereby you have no say in what those animals want to eat and what do they deem agreeable, but you have a say in whether you allow them to go there or not.

63
00:06:13,518 --> 00:06:17,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, being developed in regard to the animals means being developed in regard to the body.

64
00:06:17,919 --> 00:06:26,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when those animals do experience pleasure, or look, they saw something exciting, if you're developed in regard to the body, means, eh...

65
00:06:27,366 --> 00:06:29,948
[SPEAKER_01]: The excitement of those animals cannot overwhelm you.

66
00:06:30,568 --> 00:06:33,250
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, they cannot take charge.

67
00:06:33,511 --> 00:06:39,916
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they do see something, or hear something, or touch something agreeable, you see it for what it is.

68
00:06:40,516 --> 00:06:44,119
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an agreeable thing of these animals that they would want to partake in.

69
00:06:44,859 --> 00:06:46,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't lose your perspective.

70
00:06:46,881 --> 00:06:52,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you allow the animals to partake in it or not, is determined by the wisdom of your perspective.

71
00:06:53,562 --> 00:06:54,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

72
00:06:54,382 --> 00:06:57,944
[SPEAKER_01]: The clearer that becomes, the easier the sensory strain is.

73
00:06:59,284 --> 00:07:03,826
[SPEAKER_01]: But fundamentally, it has to be dealt with, in that sense.

74
00:07:03,886 --> 00:07:19,973
[SPEAKER_01]: The sensory strain needs to be developed, because you cannot even fathom being developed in the body, which means not being moved by the prospects of pleasure, or threats of pain, remaining equanimous towards it,

75
00:07:20,903 --> 00:07:25,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Without having invested effort in sensory strain beforehand, that's inconceivable.

76
00:07:35,588 --> 00:07:44,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of pain, painful feeling overwhelms... Well, pain, pain is... like, if you're overwhelmed by the pain, means now that's the more on the mental side.

77
00:07:46,271 --> 00:07:48,112
[SPEAKER_01]: But, but fundamentally, it's like, see,

78
00:07:49,145 --> 00:07:52,688
[SPEAKER_01]: When pleasure touches you, not having enough of pleasure, it's painful.

79
00:07:53,569 --> 00:07:57,892
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, if you want to get rid of that pain, which is not physical now, obviously.

80
00:07:58,172 --> 00:08:00,074
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not pertaining to the body.

81
00:08:00,114 --> 00:08:02,236
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to that which body lacks.

82
00:08:02,376 --> 00:08:06,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's now your recognition of not having enough, for example, of sense objects that you want.

83
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:08,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you suffer.

84
00:08:08,221 --> 00:08:08,801
[SPEAKER_01]: That's mental.

85
00:08:09,962 --> 00:08:13,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Not wanting to get rid of that mental

86
00:08:14,472 --> 00:08:20,637
[SPEAKER_01]: That feeling that has arisen as a result of your mental assumptions and expectations, oh, you're not developed in the mind.

87
00:08:21,878 --> 00:08:26,101
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the pleasure touches you, on the level of the body, if that overwhelms you, you're not developed in the body.

88
00:08:27,142 --> 00:08:39,652
[SPEAKER_01]: When the pain touches you, on the level of the mind, expectations, wanting this, not wanting that, as the Guru would say, being paired with things you don't want and being separated from things you want, it's fundamentally the basis of any dukkha.

89
00:08:41,437 --> 00:08:44,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is a... that is a mental attitude, what you want.

90
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:53,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like in sensuality, say, you let the animals enjoy sensuality, and your mind now wants more of it.

91
00:08:53,664 --> 00:08:56,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Animals still are occupied with what they're eating, tasting, touching.

92
00:08:57,987 --> 00:09:03,210
[SPEAKER_01]: But your recognition, your loss of right perspective, sees that there could be more of this.

93
00:09:03,750 --> 00:09:08,613
[SPEAKER_01]: And now that's the mental pain, in regard to not having enough for what you took for granted.

94
00:09:14,283 --> 00:09:15,903
[SPEAKER_01]: So, developed in the mind, developed in the body.

95
00:09:16,183 --> 00:09:17,884
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle of development, by the way.

96
00:09:17,904 --> 00:09:19,504
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like these two separate things.

97
00:09:20,444 --> 00:09:41,168
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are developing yourself in the body, by sort of not being overwhelmed by the prospects of pleasure that manifests in the body, you are developing yourself in the mind, because part of that development is not to resist that painful feeling of restraint.

98
00:09:43,038 --> 00:09:45,040
[SPEAKER_01]: See for what it is, not try to get rid of it.

99
00:09:47,021 --> 00:09:50,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you practice development in the body, in the body your mind will come along.

100
00:09:51,465 --> 00:09:53,987
[SPEAKER_01]: If you practice development in the mind, your body will come along.

101
00:10:03,374 --> 00:10:06,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you develop yourself in the mind?

102
00:10:07,097 --> 00:10:09,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let's say something upsets you today.

103
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:11,401
[SPEAKER_01]: There is the unpleasant feeling enduring.

104
00:10:12,988 --> 00:10:14,269
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your attitude towards it?

105
00:10:16,711 --> 00:10:17,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's mental fear.

106
00:10:17,452 --> 00:10:18,653
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not bodily.

107
00:10:19,574 --> 00:10:20,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Your senses are calm.

108
00:10:21,455 --> 00:10:29,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes, you know, you're not agitated because you're looking at something disagreeable, or hearing something that's really unpleasant, or burning with some painful sense of touch.

109
00:10:30,723 --> 00:10:33,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, bodily, you're fine, but you're not fine.

110
00:10:33,805 --> 00:10:36,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Because something did upset you, and your mind revolves around it.

111
00:10:38,049 --> 00:10:40,291
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is your attitude towards that displeasure?

112
00:10:41,861 --> 00:10:43,202
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's trying to get rid of it...

113
00:10:43,783 --> 00:10:44,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there you go.

114
00:10:44,443 --> 00:10:46,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means that you're already kind of...

115
00:10:46,185 --> 00:10:49,388
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're trying to get rid of it, means you don't want to see it for what it is.

116
00:10:49,448 --> 00:11:04,061
[SPEAKER_01]: You just want it to not be there, because you basically act entitled as if you have a right to not experience these things, which means you don't really understand the mind, and you're certainly trying to take control of it in that appropriated sense.

117
00:11:04,121 --> 00:11:04,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is mine.

118
00:11:04,882 --> 00:11:06,163
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want this to be here.

119
00:11:06,303 --> 00:11:07,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm the ruler-controller here.

120
00:11:08,503 --> 00:11:13,227
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were the ruler-controller, these things would have not arisen in the first place, things you have to deal with.

121
00:11:13,828 --> 00:11:16,850
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can be, you can, in a way, be the master.

122
00:11:17,491 --> 00:11:21,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Be the master by removing the ownership that you don't have, to begin with.

123
00:11:21,915 --> 00:11:27,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be the master by stop borrowing money from others and building your kingdom on top of that.

124
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:32,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when they're coming to collect what you owe them, you have to give them your kingdom.

125
00:11:32,164 --> 00:11:33,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you were not a master ever.

126
00:11:34,913 --> 00:11:40,075
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you build your kingdom not on borrowed gold and valuables, then nobody can take that away.

127
00:11:43,816 --> 00:11:54,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you develop yourself in the mind by, first and foremost, not resisting that mental field, not trying to get rid of it.

128
00:11:54,020 --> 00:12:03,103
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... you start developing yourself in the body by being sensory-strained, then the mind will start to rebel against it,

129
00:12:04,616 --> 00:12:08,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strain will be felt unpleasantly, not enough pleasure.

130
00:12:08,998 --> 00:12:10,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It will all be felt on a mental level.

131
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:15,262
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't resist that now, you're now developing yourself in the mind.

132
00:12:18,063 --> 00:12:20,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might ask, how long, how long do I have to not resist that?

133
00:12:21,445 --> 00:12:22,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Indefinitely.

134
00:12:23,546 --> 00:12:25,707
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the attitude that needs to be developed.

135
00:12:26,327 --> 00:12:32,871
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, I'll do this for a week, then all the problems will be sorted, and then I'll go back to my life as an enlightened person.

136
00:12:33,091 --> 00:12:33,271
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

137
00:12:34,417 --> 00:12:39,060
[SPEAKER_01]: You are reshaping, pretty much, the way you exist.

138
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:40,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the way you exist is flawed.

139
00:12:41,481 --> 00:12:52,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's based on anicca, it's based on appropriation, it's based on gratuitous ownership, upādāna, holding, all these things that one must get rid of if one wants to be free from suffering.

140
00:12:55,208 --> 00:12:58,090
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to have the attitude of, I'll never resist pain.

141
00:13:01,274 --> 00:13:04,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean like I'll never move if my body aches physically.

142
00:13:05,236 --> 00:13:13,599
[SPEAKER_01]: But mentally, when there is any form of mental discomfort, I will not try to get rid of it for its own sake.

143
00:13:15,439 --> 00:13:24,523
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you want to be practicing how to be unmoved, unpressured by the pressure, by things that pressure you.

144
00:13:25,563 --> 00:13:27,364
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, your lifestyle needs to change.

145
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:33,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strength, virtue, sīla, and so on, as we covered in so many previous talks.

146
00:13:34,922 --> 00:13:42,905
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, on top of that, when you do experience this discomfort of the mental kind, mind resisting, that's where the work is.

147
00:13:43,065 --> 00:13:45,726
[SPEAKER_01]: The untrained mind resists, like a wild monkey.

148
00:13:46,286 --> 00:13:48,106
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want this, and he kicks and screams.

149
00:13:48,767 --> 00:13:50,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Even more than the other senses do.

150
00:13:51,127 --> 00:13:53,088
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, the mind is the hardest to tame.

151
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:57,970
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't tame it.

152
00:13:58,866 --> 00:14:08,589
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have set up some perimeter of sense restraint, so there is no spilling over that, so your behavior is kept in check, and then, yeah, things will come up.

153
00:14:08,649 --> 00:14:09,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

154
00:14:09,349 --> 00:14:13,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even need to be seeking out things to disturb yourself with, to practice.

155
00:14:13,890 --> 00:14:19,572
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is restrain your other senses, and that's already going to be a disturbance for a mind that's not free from sensuality.

156
00:14:20,831 --> 00:14:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is don't go and seek company and careless chit-chat and pointless talk.

157
00:14:26,195 --> 00:14:29,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Live in solitude for somebody who hasn't developed that.

158
00:14:29,378 --> 00:14:30,959
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already going to be a disturbance.

159
00:14:31,099 --> 00:14:32,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind will already be resisting that.

160
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:36,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Because mind wants things more, depends on engagement and so on.

161
00:14:39,812 --> 00:14:43,594
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel the vacuum that you've... you know, this craving.

162
00:14:44,135 --> 00:14:44,955
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, yeah.

163
00:14:44,995 --> 00:14:46,356
[SPEAKER_01]: The craving needs to be maintained.

164
00:14:46,376 --> 00:14:49,198
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop maintaining it, it's going to be felt like a vacuum.

165
00:14:49,578 --> 00:14:54,841
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, if you sustain it, if you don't break the precepts, that vacuum will evaporate.

166
00:14:55,782 --> 00:14:57,143
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the pressure will go with it.

167
00:14:57,764 --> 00:15:03,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will actually start preferring solitude, preferring sense restraint, preferring non-disturbance.

168
00:15:03,628 --> 00:15:09,573
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will have no problem, when unpleasant feeling arises in your mind, to not resist it.

169
00:15:10,074 --> 00:15:10,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why?

170
00:15:11,675 --> 00:15:14,557
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, because now you know it as impermanent.

171
00:15:15,078 --> 00:15:16,559
[SPEAKER_01]: You see it as unownable.

172
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:20,022
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's more than enough for you to not suffer on account of it.

173
00:15:20,872 --> 00:15:27,956
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people keep saying, oh yeah, it's impermanent, it's impermanent, yet they still suffer, means they don't see impermanence for what it is.

174
00:15:28,257 --> 00:15:33,960
